{"model__predicts__probability_of_surviving_through_tj_for_individual_i": "The predicate \"predicts\" signifies the model's function of generating an estimate or forecast. In this context, it specifically refers to the model's capability to output a probability value based on input data concerning individual survival rates over time."},
{"probability_of_surviving_through_tj_for_individual_i": "The object \"probability of surviving through tj for individual i\" represents the specific output of the model. It is a quantitative measure indicating the likelihood that a particular individual (denoted as i) will survive up to a certain time point (tj). This probability is crucial for assessing survival outcomes in the given context."},
{"uncensored_individuals": "Uncensored individuals refer to those in a study or dataset for whom the event of interest (such as failure or death) has been observed. In the context of survival analysis, these are individuals whose survival times are fully known because the event occurred before the study ended."},
{"uncensored_individuals__have__full_cross_entropy_loss_across_all_time_points": "The predicate \"have\" in this context signifies the possession or application of a specific loss function to uncensored individuals in a statistical or machine learning model. It indicates that a certain method or calculation is used for these individuals due to their known survival times."},
{"full_cross_entropy_loss_across_all_time_points": "Full cross-entropy loss across all time points is a method used in machine learning, particularly in survival analysis, to measure the difference between predicted survival probabilities and actual observed outcomes for all time points. It is applied to uncensored individuals because their exact survival times are known, allowing for a comprehensive assessment of the model's predictions at every time point."},
{"censored_individuals__have__partial_cross_entropy_up_to_m_i": "The predicate \"have\" in this context signifies the possession or application of a specific loss function component to censored individuals. It indicates that for these individuals, a particular method or calculation is used to determine their contribution to the overall loss function of the model."},
{"partial_cross_entropy_up_to_m_i": "Partial cross-entropy up to m(i) is a method used in the loss function for censored individuals. It calculates the cross-entropy loss only up to the largest index m(i), where the individual's survival time is known to be less than or equal to their censoring time. This approach accounts for the uncertainty in the exact survival time beyond the censoring point."},
{"m_i": "The subject, denoted as m(i), represents a specific index in the context of a survival analysis model. It is used to determine the extent of the model's prediction for an individual's survival probability up to a certain time point."},
{"m_i__is__largest_index_such_that_tm_i_t_i": "The predicate \"is\" serves as an equal sign in this context, establishing the relationship between the subject m(i) and the object, which is the largest index such that tm(i) \u2264 T[\u02dc]i. It indicates that m(i) is defined as this largest index."},
{"largest_index_such_that_tm_i_t_i": "The object, \"largest index such that tm(i) \u2264 T[\u02dc]i,\" refers to the maximum index value for which the survival time tm(i) does not exceed the censoring time T[\u02dc]i for individual i. This index is crucial for applying a partial cross-entropy loss function in the survival analysis model."},
{"yi_j_log_p_\u02c6_i_j_1_yi_j_log_1_p_\u02c6_i_j": "The subject represents a mathematical expression involving logarithmic terms and variables, specifically _yi,j_ and _p_ \u02c6 _i,j_. This expression is part of a larger equation or formula."},
