{"hyperparameter": "The hyperparameter in this context refers to a variable, specifically denoted as \u03b1, that is used to combine individual losses from a dataset into a single loss function. It plays a crucial role in adjusting the balance or weight of the combined loss."},
{"hyperparameter__is__\u03b1_0_1": "The predicate \"is\" in this triple signifies that the hyperparameter \u03b1 is defined or characterized by the range [0, 1]. This means that the value of \u03b1 must fall within this interval to be valid for the given application."},
{"\u03b1_0_1": "The object \u03b1 \u2208 [0, 1] describes the range within which the hyperparameter \u03b1 must lie. This interval includes all real numbers from 0 to 1, inclusive, indicating that \u03b1 can take any value between these two bounds to appropriately scale or weight the combined loss function."},
{"\u03b4i__is_equal_to__1_1_\u03b1": "The predicate \"is equal to\" indicates a relationship of equality between the subject \u03b4i and the object 1 + (1 \u2212 \u03b1), meaning that \u03b4i has the same value as the expression 1 + (1 \u2212 \u03b1)."},
{"1_1_\u03b1": "1 + (1 \u2212 \u03b1) is an algebraic expression involving the variable \u03b1. It represents a value that is calculated by adding 1 to the result of subtracting \u03b1 from 1."},
