{"tuning_\u03b1": "Tuning _\u03b1_ refers to the process of adjusting the parameter _\u03b1_ in a statistical model to achieve a desired balance. This adjustment is crucial for optimizing the model's performance in handling different types of data."},
{"tuning_\u03b1__balances__contribution_of_uncensored_and_censored_observations": "The predicate \"balances\" indicates the action of achieving equilibrium or harmony between two or more elements. In this context, it signifies the act of adjusting _\u03b1_ to create a fair and effective contribution from both uncensored and censored observations."},
{"contribution_of_uncensored_and_censored_observations": "The contribution of uncensored and censored observations refers to the impact or influence that both types of data have on the model's predictions. Uncensored observations are complete data points, while censored observations are incomplete or partially observed data points. Balancing their contributions ensures that the model performs well across the entire dataset.|Here is another description: The contribution of uncensored and censored observations refers to the balance achieved by tuning the parameter _\u03b1_, which considers both types of data in the analysis."},
{"contribution_of_uncensored_and_censored_observations__leads_to__more_robust_survival_predictions": "Leads to indicates the result or outcome that is achieved by balancing the contribution of uncensored and censored observations through the tuning of _\u03b1_."},
{"more_robust_survival_predictions": "More robust survival predictions are the enhanced and reliable forecasts of survival outcomes that are obtained by effectively balancing the contributions of uncensored and censored observations in the study population.|Here is another description: More robust survival predictions refer to enhanced and reliable estimates of patient survival times, achieved through a balanced consideration of both uncensored and censored observations in the analysis."},
{"more_robust_survival_predictions__across__entire_study_population": "The predicate \"across\" indicates the scope or range over which the subject, more robust survival predictions, is applied or effective. In this context, it signifies that these predictions are relevant and applicable throughout the entire study population."},
{"entire_study_population": "The entire study population represents the complete group of individuals included in the research, whose survival outcomes are being predicted. This population encompasses all participants, regardless of whether their survival times are fully observed (uncensored) or partially observed (censored)."},
{"individual_donor_recipient_pair": "An individual donor-recipient pair refers to a specific combination of a donor and a recipient in the context of organ transplantation or similar medical procedures. This pair is evaluated based on various factors to determine the suitability of the donor for the recipient."},
{"individual_donor_recipient_pair__has__compatibility_score": "The predicate \"has\" in this context signifies the possession or calculation of a specific metric or score that is relevant to the donor-recipient pair. It indicates that a certain value or assessment is associated with this pair."},
{"compatibility_score": "A compatibility score is a numerical value that quantifies the compatibility or suitability of a donor for a recipient in a medical context. This score is derived from various factors, such as survival probabilities and expected survival times, to provide an overall assessment of the donor-recipient match.|Here is another description: The compatibility score is a metric used to evaluate the suitability of a donor-recipient pair in a medical context, specifically in the context of organ transplantation. It is derived from the expected survival time of the recipient, which is calculated using survival probabilities at discrete time points.|Here is another description: The compatibility score is a metric used to evaluate the compatibility between a donor and a recipient in a specific context, such as organ transplantation. It is calculated based on survival probabilities predicted by a trained model."},
{"compatibility_score__is_computed_by__obtaining_estimates_of_the_survival_probability": "The compatibility score is computed by a process that involves obtaining estimates of the survival probability at various time points. This computation is crucial as it integrates these probabilities to approximate the expected survival time, which directly influences the compatibility score."},
{"obtaining_estimates_of_the_survival_probability": "Obtaining estimates of the survival probability involves using a trained XGBoost survival model to predict the survival probability at discrete time points. These estimates are essential for calculating the expected survival time and, consequently, the compatibility score for a donor-recipient pair."},
{"survival_probability": "The survival probability refers to the likelihood of an individual surviving past a certain time point. In the context of donor-recipient compatibility, it is a critical factor used to estimate the expected survival time and overall compatibility score.|Here is another description: The survival probability is a measure used to estimate the likelihood of an individual surviving past a certain time point. In the context of donor-recipient compatibility, it is crucial for predicting outcomes and making informed decisions.|Here is another description: The survival probability refers to the likelihood of an individual surviving past a certain time point. In the context of the XGBoost survival model, it is estimated at discrete time points and used to compute a compatibility score for donor-recipient pairs."},
{"survival_probability__is_obtained_using__trained_xgboost_survival_model": "The phrase \"is obtained using\" indicates the method or tool employed to derive the survival probability. Here, it specifies that the survival probability estimates are generated through the application of a trained XGBoost survival model."},
{"trained_xgboost_survival_model": "The trained XGBoost survival model is a machine learning model that has been specifically trained to predict survival probabilities. It is utilized in this context to provide accurate estimates of survival probabilities at discrete time points for donor-recipient pairs."},
{"survival_probability__is_represented_as__si_tj_pij": "The phrase \"is represented as\" indicates that the survival probability is mathematically expressed or symbolized in a specific form. This representation allows for quantitative analysis and computation of survival outcomes over time."},
{"si_tj_pij": "Si(tj) = pij is a mathematical representation where Si denotes the survival probability for individual i at time point j, and pij is the estimated probability derived from a trained model, such as XGBoost. This equation is used to predict survival probabilities at discrete time intervals."},
{"expected_survival_time": "The expected survival time refers to the average duration that a subject is predicted to survive based on certain survival probabilities at discrete time points.|Here is another description: The expected survival time is a statistical measure that represents the average time an individual is expected to survive from a certain point, often used in medical and actuarial sciences to predict longevity based on various factors."},
{"expected_survival_time__is_approximated_by__discretely_integrating_the_survival_curve": "The phrase \"is approximated by\" indicates that the expected survival time is estimated using a specific method or calculation, in this case, by discretely integrating the survival curve."},
{"discretely_integrating_the_survival_curve": "Discretely integrating the survival curve involves summing the survival probabilities at discrete time points, assuming the survival probability is constant within each interval, to approximate the expected survival time."},
{"starting_time_point": "The starting time point refers to the initial moment in a time series or sequence where measurements or observations begin. In the context of survival analysis, it marks the beginning of the period during which survival probabilities are estimated."},
{"starting_time_point__is__t0_0": "The predicate \"is\" establishes an identity or definition between the subject and the object. It indicates that the starting time point is specifically defined as a certain value or condition, in this case, t0 = 0."},
{"t0_0": "t0 = 0 represents the initial time point in a sequence or series, specifically set to zero. This value serves as the reference point from which subsequent time intervals and survival probabilities are calculated and measured."},
{"starting_survival_probability": "The starting survival probability refers to the initial point in time, denoted as t0, where the survival probability is considered to be at its maximum, which is 1. This represents the beginning of the survival curve in the context of a donor-recipient compatibility assessment."},
{"starting_survival_probability__is__si_t0_1": "The predicate \"is\" in this context signifies the assignment or definition of the starting survival probability at time t0. It establishes the baseline value for the survival probability at the onset of the survival curve."},
{"si_t0_1": "Si(t0) = 1 represents the initial survival probability at time t0, which is defined as 1. This value indicates that at the starting point of the survival curve, the probability of survival is at its highest, meaning no events (such as death or failure) have occurred yet."},
{"survival_probability__is_assumed_constant_for__each_interval_tj_tj_1": "The phrase \"is assumed constant for\" indicates that within the specified intervals, the survival probability does not change. This assumption simplifies the calculation of the expected survival time by allowing the use of a constant value for the survival probability within each interval."},
{"each_interval_tj_tj_1": "Each interval [tj, tj+1] represents a specific time period between two consecutive time points, tj and tj+1. During this interval, the survival probability is treated as constant for the purpose of calculating the incremental contribution to the expected survival time."},
{"incremental_contribution": "The incremental contribution refers to the contribution to the expected survival time within a specific time interval [tj, tj+1]. It is a component used in the calculation of the overall compatibility score for an individual donor-recipient pair."},
{"incremental_contribution__is_represented_as__si_tj_1_tj_1_tj": "The phrase \"is represented as\" indicates the mathematical expression used to quantify the incremental contribution within a given time interval. It shows how the incremental contribution is calculated using the survival probability and the length of the time interval."},
{"si_tj_1_tj_1_tj": "The expression Si(tj+1) \u00b7 (tj+1 \u2212 tj) represents the incremental contribution for a specific time interval [tj, tj+1]. It is the product of the survival probability at the end of the interval, Si(tj+1), and the length of the interval, (tj+1 \u2212 tj). This expression is used to approximate the contribution to the expected survival time within that interval."},
{"compatibility_score__is_named__discscore_i": "The predicate \"is named\" indicates that the compatibility score has been given a specific designation or label. In this case, the score is referred to as DisCScore(i), which signifies its unique identification for individual i."},
{"discscore_i": "DisCScore(i) is the specific name given to the compatibility score for individual i. It represents the calculated score that reflects the expected survival time and compatibility between a donor and recipient pair, based on the survival probabilities predicted by the XGBoost survival model."},
{"si": "Si represents a variable or a term in a mathematical expression, likely used to denote a specific quantity or value within the context of the equation."},
