{"we__followed__the_data_cleaning_methodology_proposed_by_berrevoets_et_al_18": "The predicate \"followed\" indicates that the subject adhered to or implemented a specific methodology in their research process."},
{"the_data_cleaning_methodology_proposed_by_berrevoets_et_al_18": "The object \"the data cleaning methodology proposed by Berrevoets _et al._ [18]\" refers to a specific set of procedures and guidelines for data cleaning that were previously established and published by Berrevoets and colleagues in their work, which is cited as reference [18]."},
{"we__refined__the_feature_set": "The predicate \"refined\" indicates the action taken by the subject to improve or make more precise the feature set through a series of assessments and expert reviews."},
{"the_feature_set": "The object \"the feature set\" represents the collection of variables or attributes that were subjected to refinement, including the removal of features with high missingness or low variability, and the inclusion of clinically important features as determined by expert clinicians."},
{"refinement": "The refinement refers to the process of improving or enhancing the feature set used in a study or analysis. This process is crucial for ensuring that the features selected are relevant, reliable, and contribute meaningfully to the outcomes of the study."},
{"refinement__was_done_through__a_series_of_statistical_assessments": "The phrase \"was done through\" indicates the method or means by which the refinement of the feature set was achieved. It suggests a procedural approach involving specific techniques or steps to accomplish the refinement."},
{"a_series_of_statistical_assessments": "A series of statistical assessments involves the application of various statistical methods and tests to evaluate the features within the dataset. These assessments help in identifying features that are either redundant, irrelevant, or do not contribute significantly to the analysis, thereby aiding in the refinement process."},
{"features__were_removed_if__they_had_more_than_20_missingness_or_exhibited_zero_or_near_zero_variability": "The predicate \"were removed if\" signifies a conditional action taken during the data cleaning process. It indicates that certain features were excluded from the dataset based on specific criteria related to their data quality and variability."},
{"they_had_more_than_20_missingness_or_exhibited_zero_or_near_zero_variability": "The object describes the specific conditions under which features were removed: having more than 20% missingness or exhibiting zero or near-zero variability. These conditions suggest that the features lacked sufficient data or did not provide meaningful variation, making them unsuitable for analysis."},
{"expert_clinicians__reviewed__the_remaining_variables": "The act of reviewing by expert clinicians involves a thorough examination and assessment of medical data, variables, or procedures to ensure their clinical relevance, accuracy, and appropriateness. This process helps in identifying important features and discarding irrelevant ones, ultimately contributing to improved patient care and outcomes."},
{"the_remaining_variables": "The remaining variables refer to the set of features or data points that have passed initial filters, such as those with less than 20% missingness and non-zero variability. These variables are then subjected to further scrutiny by expert clinicians to determine their clinical importance and suitability for inclusion in the study or analysis."},
{"expert_clinicians__identified__clinically_important_features": "The act of identifying involves carefully examining and selecting relevant elements from a set of data. In this context, expert clinicians used their expertise to discern which features were significant for clinical purposes."},
{"clinically_important_features": "Clinically important features are specific variables or characteristics within medical data that have a meaningful impact on patient outcomes or diagnostic processes. These features are critical for making informed clinical decisions."},
{"expert_clinicians__discarded__irrelevant_ones": "The predicate \"discarded\" indicates the action taken by the expert clinicians to remove certain elements from consideration. In this context, it refers to the process where clinicians reviewed the variables and decided to eliminate those deemed irrelevant to the study's objectives."},
{"irrelevant_ones": "Irrelevan ones refer to the variables or features that were deemed not clinically significant or useful for the study. These are the elements that expert clinicians decided to remove from the dataset during the feature selection process to ensure that only pertinent and valuable data were included in the analysis."},
{"the_full_inclusion_and_exclusion_criteria": "The full inclusion and exclusion criteria refer to the comprehensive set of rules and conditions that determine which study samples and features are selected for further analysis in a research study. These criteria are essential for ensuring that the data used is relevant, reliable, and appropriate for the study's objectives."},
{"the_full_inclusion_and_exclusion_criteria__are_shown_in__supplementary_a": "The phrase \"are shown in\" indicates that the detailed information about the inclusion and exclusion criteria can be found within a specified document or section. This predicate serves as a reference point, directing readers to the exact location where the criteria are explicitly outlined."},
{"supplementary_a": "Supplementary A is an additional document or section that accompanies the main text of a research paper or report. It provides extra information, data, or details that support the main content but are too extensive or tangential to include in the primary document. In this case, Supplementary A contains the detailed inclusion and exclusion criteria for the study."},
{"c_index": "The C-index, also known as Harrell\u2019s Concordance Index, is a widely used metric in the field of survival analysis. It is employed to assess the discriminative ability of a model in predicting which patient will experience an event, such as disease progression, sooner than another."},
{"c_index__is_a_metric_for__evaluating_a_model_s_discriminative_ability_in_survival_analysis": "The predicate \"is a metric for\" signifies that the C-index serves as a quantitative measure. Specifically, it evaluates how effectively a model can distinguish between patients based on their predicted survival times, indicating the model's capability to make accurate predictions."},
{"evaluating_a_model_s_discriminative_ability_in_survival_analysis": "Evaluating a model\u2019s discriminative ability in survival analysis refers to the process of assessing how well a model can differentiate between patients in terms of their survival outcomes. This involves determining the model's accuracy in predicting which patient is likely to experience an event, such as death or disease recurrence, earlier than another."},
{"values_of_c_index_near_0_5": "Values of C-index near 0.5 refer to a specific range of the Harrell\u2019s Concordance Index, a metric used in survival analysis to evaluate a model\u2019s ability to discriminate between patients based on their predicted survival times."},
{"values_of_c_index_near_0_5__indicate__that_the_score_predictions_are_no_better_than_a_coin_flip_in_determining_which_patient_will_live_longer": "The predicate \"indicate\" in this context means to serve as a sign or signal that conveys information about the effectiveness of the model\u2019s predictions. Specifically, it highlights the performance level of the model in terms of its discriminative ability."},
{"that_the_score_predictions_are_no_better_than_a_coin_flip_in_determining_which_patient_will_live_longer": "The object \"that the score predictions are no better than a coin flip in determining which patient will live longer\" describes a scenario where the model\u2019s predictions are as accurate as random chance. This implies that the model does not provide any meaningful insight or advantage in predicting patient outcomes compared to a simple random guess."},
{"values_of_c_index_near_1": "Values of C-index near 1 refer to a high score in Harrell\u2019s Concordance Index, which is a metric used in survival analysis to evaluate a model\u2019s ability to discriminate between patients based on their likelihood of experiencing an event, such as disease onset."},
{"values_of_c_index_near_1__indicate__that_the_scores_are_good_at_determining_which_of_two_patients_will_have_the_disease_first": "The predicate \"indicate\" in this context means to show or suggest a particular outcome or result. Here, it suggests that a certain level of the C-index score implies a specific level of model performance."},
{"that_the_scores_are_good_at_determining_which_of_two_patients_will_have_the_disease_first": "The object \"that the scores are good at determining which of two patients will have the disease first\" describes the outcome suggested by a high C-index value. It means that the model is effective in ranking patients based on their risk of disease onset, with higher scores correctly identifying patients who are more likely to develop the disease sooner."},
{"time_dependent_roc_curve": "The Time-Dependent ROC curve is a specific type of ROC curve used in survival analysis to evaluate a model's discriminative ability at a particular time point _t_. It is designed to assess how well the model can distinguish between individuals based on their survival outcomes up to time _t_.|Here is another description: The Time-Dependent ROC curve is a method used in survival analysis to evaluate the discriminative ability of a model at a specific time point _t_. It compares individuals who experience an event (e.g., failure) by time _t_ with those who do not, providing insights into the model's performance at that particular moment."},
{"time_dependent_roc_curve__compares__individuals_who_fail_by_t_with_those_who_survive_beyond_t": "The predicate \"compares\" in this context refers to the action of the Time-Dependent ROC curve in evaluating and contrasting the risk scores or compatibility scores of different individuals. It specifically measures how well the model can differentiate between those who fail (experience an event) by time _t_ and those who survive beyond _t_."},
{"individuals_who_fail_by_t_with_those_who_survive_beyond_t": "The object \"individuals who fail by _t_ with those who survive beyond _t_\" refers to the two groups of individuals that the Time-Dependent ROC curve evaluates. The first group consists of individuals who experience the event of interest (e.g., disease occurrence or death) by time _t_. The second group includes individuals who remain event-free beyond time _t_. The curve assesses how effectively the model can assign higher risk scores to the former group compared to the latter."},
{"time_dependent_roc_curve__measures__model_discrimination_at_t": "The predicate \"measures\" indicates the action of quantifying or assessing the discriminative ability of a model at a specific time point _t_. It signifies the evaluation process where the Time-Dependent ROC curve is used to gauge how well the model can distinguish between different outcomes at time _t_."},
{"model_discrimination_at_t": "Model discrimination at _t_ refers to the ability of a predictive model to differentiate between individuals who will experience an event by time _t_ and those who will not. This discrimination is quantified using the Time-Dependent ROC curve, which provides a measure of how well the model can predict outcomes at the specific time point _t_."},
{"auc_t": "AUC( _t_ ) is a metric used in survival analysis to evaluate a model's discriminative ability at a specific time _t_. It is derived from the time-dependent ROC curve, which compares individuals who fail by time _t_ with those who survive beyond _t_."},
{"auc_t__quantifies__the_probability_that_the_model_assigns_a_higher_risk_score_to_the_individual_who_fails_earlier": "The predicate \"quantifies\" indicates that AUC( _t_ ) provides a numerical measure of a specific aspect of the model's performance. In this context, it measures the effectiveness of the model in distinguishing between individuals based on their risk scores at time _t_."},
{"the_probability_that_the_model_assigns_a_higher_risk_score_to_the_individual_who_fails_earlier": "The object \"the probability that the model assigns a higher risk score to the individual who fails earlier\" refers to the likelihood that the model correctly identifies individuals who will experience an event (such as disease onset) sooner by assigning them a higher risk score. This probability is a key indicator of the model's discriminative ability at a given time _t_."},
{"auc_t_of_0_5": "AUC( _t_ ) of 0.5 refers to the Area Under the Curve at a specific time _t_, which is a measure used in survival analysis to evaluate a model's discriminative ability at that particular time point. An AUC( _t_ ) value of 0.5 suggests that the model's predictions are equivalent to random guessing at time _t_."},
{"auc_t_of_0_5__indicates__random_guessing_at_time_t": "The predicate \"indicates\" in this context signifies that the value of AUC( _t_ ) at 0.5 serves as an indicator or signal of the model's performance. Specifically, it points to the level of discriminative ability the model has at time _t_, suggesting whether the model's predictions are better than, equivalent to, or worse than random guessing."},
{"random_guessing_at_time_t": "Random guessing at time _t_ refers to a scenario where a model's predictions are no better than those made by chance at a specific time _t_. In the context of survival analysis and the evaluation of discriminative ability, this means that the model does not have any meaningful ability to distinguish between individuals who will fail by time _t_ and those who will survive beyond _t_."},
{"auc_t_near_1_0": "AUC( _t_ ) near 1.0 refers to the area under the time-dependent ROC curve at a specific time _t_, which is a measure used in survival analysis to evaluate the discriminative ability of a model at that particular time."},
{"auc_t_near_1_0__signifies__strong_discriminative_ability": "The predicate \"signifies\" in this context means that the value of AUC( _t_ ) near 1.0 is an indicator or a clear signal of a certain characteristic or quality of the model's performance."},
{"strong_discriminative_ability": "Strong discriminative ability refers to the capability of a model to effectively distinguish between different classes or outcomes, in this case, the ability to accurately predict which individuals will fail (experience an event) by time _t_ versus those who will survive beyond _t_."},
