{"iterative_improvement": "Iterative improvement refers to a process where each step or iteration is designed to enhance the performance or accuracy of a model by learning from the errors of the previous iteration. In the context of machine learning models like XGBoost, this approach involves building each new model to correct the mistakes of the preceding one, leading to gradual enhancements in model performance."},
{"iterative_improvement__yields__more_accurate_models_than_bagging_methods": "The predicate \"yields\" in this context signifies the outcome or result produced by the process of iterative improvement. It indicates that through the continuous refinement and correction of errors in each iteration, a certain beneficial effect or advantage is achieved, specifically in terms of model accuracy."},
{"more_accurate_models_than_bagging_methods": "More accurate models than bagging methods refer to machine learning models that, through the process of iterative improvement, achieve higher predictive accuracy compared to models that use bagging techniques. Bagging methods, such as Random Forest, involve training multiple models independently and then aggregating their predictions, whereas iterative improvement focuses on building upon and correcting the errors of previous models to enhance accuracy."},
{"bagging_methods": "Bagging methods are ensemble learning techniques that involve training multiple models independently and then aggregating their predictions to improve overall performance. They are often used to reduce variance and prevent overfitting in machine learning models."},
{"bagging_methods__include__random_forest": "The predicate \"include\" signifies that the subject, bagging methods, encompasses or contains the object, Random Forest, as one of its specific instances or applications."},
{"random_forest": "Random Forest is a popular ensemble learning method that operates by constructing a multitude of decision trees during training and outputting the class that is the mode of the classes of the individual trees. It is known for its robustness and ability to handle high-dimensional data."},
{"all_trees_in_bagging_methods": "All trees in bagging methods refer to the individual decision trees that are part of ensemble learning techniques like Random Forest. Each tree is constructed based on a subset of the data, contributing to the overall model's predictions."},
{"all_trees_in_bagging_methods__are_trained__independently": "The phrase \"are trained\" indicates the process by which each tree in the bagging method learns from the data. Training involves adjusting the tree's parameters to minimize prediction errors on the training dataset."},
{"independently": "\"Independently\" describes the manner in which each tree in bagging methods is trained. Unlike boosting methods where trees are trained sequentially with each tree attempting to correct the errors of the previous one, in bagging, each tree is trained without reference to the others, ensuring diversity in the ensemble."},
{"prediction_model__is_detailed_on__discrete_time_points": "The phrase \"is detailed on\" indicates that the technical specifics and operational framework of the prediction model are thoroughly explained and elaborated upon within the context provided, specifically focusing on its application to discrete time points."},
{"discrete_time_points": "Discrete time points refer to specific, separate moments in time at which the prediction model makes its survival predictions. These points are crucial for the model's application in survival analysis, allowing it to assess survival outcomes at distinct intervals rather than continuously.|Here is another description: Discrete time points are specific, separate moments in time at which measurements or evaluations are made. In this context, they are the particular instances when survival probabilities are calculated and considered.|Here is another description: Discrete time points refer to specific, distinct moments in time that are part of the dataset. These time points are used to track or measure changes or events over time, providing a temporal dimension to the data."},
{"construction": "The construction refers to the process detailed in the technical framework of the approach to donor-recipient matching. It involves adapting and utilizing the multi-label XGBoost model for survival analysis, named _Disc-_ _SurvBoost_, to predict discrete binary survivals."},
{"construction__describes__the_score_capable_of_identifying_the_most_compatible_donor": "The predicate \"describes\" indicates that the construction process is explained or detailed in the context provided. It outlines how the model is used to generate a score for matching donors and recipients."},
{"the_score_capable_of_identifying_the_most_compatible_donor": "The score capable of identifying the most compatible donor is a numerical value generated by the _Disc-_ _SurvBoost_ model. This score is based on the model\u2019s predictions at each discrete time point and is used to determine the best match between a donor and a transplant recipient."},
{"score__is_based_on__the_model_s_prediction_on_each_discrete_time_point": "The predicate \"is based on\" indicates that the score is derived from or relies upon the model\u2019s predictions. This relationship signifies that the accuracy and effectiveness of the score are contingent on the quality and precision of the model\u2019s predictions at each discrete time point."},
{"the_model_s_prediction_on_each_discrete_time_point": "The object \"the model\u2019s prediction on each discrete time point\" refers to the output generated by the _Disc-_ _SurvBoost_ model at specific intervals. These predictions are binary and indicate whether an individual is likely to survive at each time point, which are then used to construct the compatibility score for donor-recipient matching."},
{"donor_recipient_pairs": "Donor-recipient pairs refer to the matched individuals involved in a medical procedure where one person donates an organ or tissue to another person who receives it. These pairs are crucial in studies evaluating the outcomes and survival rates post-transplantation."},
{"donor_recipient_pairs__experience__the_event_death": "The predicate \"experience\" in this context signifies that the donor-recipient pairs undergo or are subject to a particular event or outcome during the course of the study. It indicates the occurrence of an event that is being tracked or measured."},
{"the_event_death": "The event (death) refers to the specific outcome being observed in the study, which is the death of either the donor or the recipient within the donor-recipient pairs. This event is a critical endpoint in survival analysis, particularly in medical research evaluating the success and longevity of transplants."},
{"the_study": "The study refers to a research project or investigation that involves observing donor-recipient pairs over a period of time to determine the occurrence of an event, such as death."},
{"the_study__result__censored_observations": "The result predicate indicates the outcome or consequence of the study, specifically that not all donor-recipient pairs experienced the event by the end of the study period."},
{"censored_observations": "Censored observations refer to the data points in the study where the event of interest, such as death, did not occur by the end of the observation period. These observations are considered incomplete or censored because the exact time of the event is unknown."},
{"individual_i": "The subject, individual _i_, refers to a specific participant in a study where survival times are being observed. This individual is part of a donor-recipient pair whose outcomes are tracked over time.|Here is another description: The subject, individual _i_, refers to a specific participant or entity within a study or dataset where survival times are observed. Each individual is uniquely identified and tracked throughout the study."},
{"individual_i__have__observed_survival_time_t_i": "The predicate \"have\" indicates the possession or association of the observed survival time with the individual. It signifies that the individual _i_ is linked to a specific survival time measurement."},
{"observed_survival_time_t_i": "The object, observed survival time _T_ [\u02dc] _i_, represents the duration for which individual _i_ has been observed to survive in the study. This time is a critical data point used to analyze the outcomes of donor-recipient pairs."},
{"individual_i__have__censorship_\u03b4i": "The predicate, have, indicates the possession or association of a particular attribute or condition. In this context, it signifies that the individual _i_ is associated with a specific status or characteristic related to the observed data."},
{"censorship_\u03b4i": "The object, censorship _\u03b4i_, represents the status of whether the survival time for individual _i_ is censored. Censorship in this context means that the exact survival time is not fully observed, often due to the study ending before the event occurs or other reasons leading to incomplete data."},
{"time_axis": "The time axis refers to the continuous range of time over which events are observed or measured in a study. In survival analysis, it represents the timeline during which subjects are monitored for the occurrence of an event, such as death."},
{"time_axis__be_discretized_into__t_1_tm": "The phrase \"be discretized into\" describes the process of dividing a continuous time axis into distinct, separate intervals. This is often done in survival analysis to simplify the computation and analysis of survival probabilities at specific time points."},
{"t_1_tm": "The object _{t_ 1 _,..., tM_ _}_ represents a set of discrete time points into which the continuous time axis has been divided. These time points are used to evaluate the survival function at specific intervals, allowing for the analysis of survival probabilities at those particular moments."},
{"yi_j": "_yi,j_ represents a binary variable that is used to indicate the presence or absence of a certain condition based on the comparison of two values, _tj_ and _T_ [\u02dc] _i_.|Here is another description: Yi,j represents the discretized values resulting from the process described. These values are specific points or intervals that have been derived from the original continuous outcomes, based on certain parameters or thresholds.|Here is another description: The subject 'yi,j' represents a discretized outcome in a process that involves categorizing or segmenting continuous data into discrete values."},
{"yi_j_____1_if_tj_t_i_otherwise_0": "The predicate '=' is used to assign a value to _yi,j_ based on the condition that _tj_ is less than _T_ [\u02dc] _i_. If the condition is true, _yi,j_ is assigned the value 1, otherwise it is assigned the value 0."},
{"1_if_tj_t_i_otherwise_0": "The object '1 if _tj <_ _T_ [\u02dc] _i_, otherwise 0' is a conditional expression that determines the value of _yi,j_. It evaluates to 1 if _tj_ is less than _T_ [\u02dc] _i_, and 0 otherwise."},
{"model__predicts__probability_of_surviving_through_tj_for_individual_i": "The predicate \"predicts\" signifies the action performed by the model, which involves calculating and outputting the estimated probability of an individual surviving through a specific time point. This prediction is crucial for assessing survival outcomes in the context of the model's application."},
{"probability_of_surviving_through_tj_for_individual_i": "The object \"probability of surviving through tj for individual i\" refers to the specific output generated by the model. It represents the likelihood that individual i will survive up to time point tj, taking into account the individual's data and the model's predictive capabilities."},
{"uncensored_individuals": "Uncensored individuals refer to those subjects in a study or dataset for whom the event of interest (such as failure or death) has been observed directly, without any form of censoring. In the context of survival analysis, these individuals have a censoring indicator (\u03b4i) equal to 1, indicating that their event times are known exactly."},
{"uncensored_individuals__have__full_cross_entropy_loss_across_all_time_points": "The predicate \"have\" in this context signifies the possession or application of a specific loss function to uncensored individuals in a statistical model. It indicates that these individuals are subject to a particular method of calculating error or loss within the model, which is crucial for the model's learning and prediction accuracy."},
{"full_cross_entropy_loss_across_all_time_points": "The full cross-entropy loss across all time points is a method used in machine learning and statistical modeling to measure the performance of a classification model whose output is a probability value between 0 and 1. In the context of survival analysis, it is applied to uncensored individuals to quantify the difference between the predicted survival probabilities at all observed time points and the actual observed outcomes."},
{"censored_individuals__have__partial_cross_entropy_up_to_m_i": "The predicate \"have\" in this context signifies the possession or application of a specific loss function characteristic to censored individuals. It indicates that due to the nature of their data (being censored), a different approach to calculating the loss function is required compared to uncensored individuals."},
{"partial_cross_entropy_up_to_m_i": "Partial cross-entropy up to m(i) is a method used in the loss function for censored individuals. It calculates the cross-entropy loss only up to the largest index m(i), where the individual's survival time is known to be less than or equal to their censoring time. This approach accounts for the uncertainty beyond the censoring time, as the exact survival time post-censoring is unknown."},
{"m_i": "The subject, m(i), represents the largest index in the context of a survival analysis model where the model predicts the probability of an individual surviving through specific time points. It is a critical parameter used to determine the extent of the cross-entropy loss calculation for censored individuals."},
{"m_i__is__largest_index_such_that_tm_i_t_i": "The predicate, \"is,\" serves as an equal sign in the context, defining m(i) as the largest index that meets a specific condition related to the survival time of an individual and their censoring time."},
{"largest_index_such_that_tm_i_t_i": "The object, \"largest index such that tm(i) \u2264 T[~]i,\" describes the condition that m(i) must satisfy. It is the highest index where the survival time tm(i) does not exceed the individual's censoring time T[~]i, which is used to apply a partial cross-entropy loss for censored data in the survival analysis model."},
{"yi_j_log_p_\u02c6_i_j": "_yi,j_ log _p_ \u02c6 _i,j_ is a term in a mathematical expression, representing the product of _yi,j_ and the logarithm of _p_ \u02c6 _i,j_. This term is part of a larger equation used in statistical or probabilistic models."},
{"yi_j_log_p_\u02c6_i_j__is_part_of__the_equation": "The phrase \"is part of\" indicates that the subject term _yi,j_ log _p_ \u02c6 _i,j_ is a component within a larger mathematical equation. This relationship highlights the subject's role as a constituent element in the overall structure of the equation."},
{"the_equation": "The equation in question is a mathematical expression that includes several terms and variables. It is likely used in the context of statistical modeling or probabilistic analysis, where _yi,j_ and _p_ \u02c6 _i,j_ are variables that contribute to the overall calculation.|Here is another description: The \"equation\" refers to the complete mathematical expression that includes various terms and components, such as yi,j log p\u0302 i,j and 1 \u2212 yi,j log 1 \u2212 p\u0302 i,j, combined to form a coherent mathematical statement.|Here is another description: The equation in question is a mathematical expression involving logarithmic terms and variables, specifically _yi,j_ and _p_ \u02c6 _i,j_, which are part of a larger formula or model."},
{"1_yi_j_log_1_p_\u02c6_i_j": "The term \"1 \u2212 yi,j log 1 \u2212 p\u0302 i,j\" represents a specific component within a larger mathematical expression, involving logarithmic functions and variables yi,j and p\u0302 i,j."},
{"1_yi_j_log_1_p_\u02c6_i_j__is_part_of__the_equation": "The phrase \"is part of\" indicates that the subject term is a constituent element within a broader mathematical equation or expression."},
{"li_\u03b4i": "_Li, \u03b4i_ represents a specific term or variable within a mathematical or statistical context, likely related to a model or equation involving probabilities or predictions."},
{"li_\u03b4i__equals__0": "The predicate \"equals\" indicates a relationship of equivalence between the subject _Li, \u03b4i_ and the object 0, suggesting that _Li, \u03b4i_ is set to or results in the value of 0 under certain conditions or within the context of the equation."},
{"the_equation__equals___": "The predicate \"equals\" signifies that the equation is set to be equivalent to a specific value or expression, in this case, the result of the subtraction operation involving _yi,j_ and _p_ \u02c6 _i,j_."},
{"_": "The object \"_\u2212_\" represents the result of the subtraction operation in the equation, which is a part of the overall expression that the equation is set equal to. This subtraction involves logarithmic terms and is crucial for the balance of the equation.|Here is another description: The subject, denoted as _\u25b7_, represents a specific step in the XGBoost training process. It is a placeholder for a detailed action or operation that is part of the algorithm's iterative learning procedure."},
{"individual_loss": "Individual loss refers to the separate loss values calculated for each data point within a dataset. These losses are essential for evaluating the performance of a model on individual instances."},
{"individual_loss__is_combined__single_loss": "The predicate \"is combined\" indicates the process of aggregating multiple individual losses into a single, unified loss value. This combination is typically achieved through a specific method or formula that incorporates a hyperparameter to balance the contributions of each individual loss."},
{"single_loss": "Single loss represents the aggregated loss value derived from combining all individual losses within a dataset. This unified loss is used to assess the overall performance of a model across the entire dataset, providing a comprehensive measure of its effectiveness."},
{"dataset": "The dataset is a collection of data points used for analysis, training, or machine learning tasks. It includes various attributes such as input features, target variables, and possibly additional metadata. Each data point may consist of a feature vector, a target variable, and an indicator variable. The dataset serves as the foundational input for algorithms to learn patterns or make predictions.|Here is another description: The Dataset is a comprehensive collection of data containing multiple donor-recipient pairs, used for analyzing and calculating various metrics such as survival probabilities at different time points."},
{"dataset__has_size__n": "The predicate \"has size\" indicates the quantity or volume of data points contained within the dataset. This attribute is crucial for understanding the scale and potential complexity of the dataset."},
{"n": "The object \"N\" represents the total number of data points or observations in the dataset. This numerical value is essential for determining the dataset's size and for parameterizing certain machine learning algorithms."},
{"hyperparameter": "The hyperparameter in this context refers to a parameter, specifically denoted as \u03b1, that is used to combine individual losses from a dataset into a single loss function. It plays a crucial role in adjusting the balance or weighting of these losses."},
{"hyperparameter__is__\u03b1_0_1": "The predicate \"is\" in this triple signifies that the hyperparameter \u03b1 is defined or characterized by the range [0, 1]. This means that the value of \u03b1 must fall within this interval to be valid for the given application."},
{"\u03b1_0_1": "The object \u03b1 \u2208 [0, 1] describes the range within which the hyperparameter \u03b1 must lie. This interval includes all real numbers from 0 to 1, inclusive, indicating that \u03b1 can take any value within this continuous range to appropriately combine the losses."},
{"\u03b4i__is_equal_to__1_1_\u03b1": "The predicate \"is equal to\" indicates a relationship of equivalence between the subject \u03b4i and the object 1 + (1 \u2212 \u03b1), meaning that \u03b4i has the same value as the expression 1 + (1 \u2212 \u03b1)."},
{"1_1_\u03b1": "1 + (1 \u2212 \u03b1) is an algebraic expression involving the variable \u03b1. It represents a quantity that is the sum of 1 and the result of subtracting \u03b1 from 1, which is then added to 1."},
{"\u03b4i__is_a_function_of__\u03b1": "\"is a function of\" indicates a dependency relationship where the value of \u03b4i is determined by the value of \u03b1 in the given equation."},
{"\u03b1": "\u03b1 is another variable or parameter in the equation, which influences the value of \u03b4i. It is part of the expression that defines \u03b4i."},
{"\u03b4i__is_equal_to__0": "The predicate \"is equal to\" indicates a relationship of equivalence between the subject and the object, meaning that the value of \u03b4i is the same as the value of the object in this context."},
{"tuning_\u03b1": "Tuning _\u03b1_ refers to the process of adjusting the parameter _\u03b1_ in a statistical model to achieve a desired balance. This adjustment is crucial in models that handle both uncensored and censored data, ensuring that the model's predictions are reliable and applicable to the entire study population.|Here is another description: Tuning _\u03b1_ refers to the process of adjusting the parameter _\u03b1_ to achieve a desired balance in a statistical model."},
{"tuning_\u03b1__balances__contribution_of_uncensored_and_censored_observations": "The predicate \"balances\" indicates the action of adjusting and harmonizing the influence of different types of observations within a statistical model. In this context, it means ensuring that both uncensored and censored observations contribute appropriately to the model's outcomes, leading to more accurate and robust predictions."},
{"contribution_of_uncensored_and_censored_observations": "The contribution of uncensored and censored observations refers to the impact that both types of data have on the statistical model\u2019s predictions. Uncensored observations provide complete information, while censored observations offer partial data. Balancing their contributions is essential for creating a model that accurately reflects the entire study population\u2019s survival patterns."},
{"tuning_\u03b1__results_in__more_robust_survival_predictions": "The phrase \"results in\" indicates that the action of tuning _\u03b1_ leads to a specific outcome or effect in the context of the model."},
{"more_robust_survival_predictions": "More robust survival predictions refer to enhanced and reliable forecasts of survival times that are less sensitive to variations in the data, particularly in the context of censored observations.|Here is another description: More robust survival predictions refer to enhanced and reliable estimates of survival probabilities, which are crucial in medical and statistical studies to better understand patient outcomes over time."},
{"more_robust_survival_predictions__are_for__the_entire_study_population": "The phrase \"are for\" indicates the intended purpose or target audience of the robust survival predictions, highlighting that these predictions are specifically designed to benefit a particular group."},
{"the_entire_study_population": "The entire study population encompasses all individuals included in a research study, making it the comprehensive group for which the survival predictions are intended to provide valuable insights and outcomes."},
{"individual_donor_recipient_pair": "An individual donor-recipient pair refers to a specific combination of a donor and a recipient in the context of organ transplantation or similar medical procedures. This pair is evaluated based on various medical and biological factors to determine the suitability of the donor for the recipient."},
{"individual_donor_recipient_pair__has__compatibility_score": "The predicate \"has\" in this context signifies the possession or calculation of a specific metric or score that quantifies the relationship between the donor and recipient. It indicates that a certain value or assessment is derived from the characteristics and compatibility of the pair."},
{"compatibility_score": "The compatibility score is a numerical value that represents the degree of compatibility between a donor and a recipient. It is calculated using a trained XGBoost survival model, which predicts survival probabilities at discrete time points. The score is derived by integrating these survival probabilities over time, providing an overall measure of how well-matched the donor and recipient are likely to be.|Here is another description: The compatibility score is a metric used to evaluate the suitability of a donor-recipient pair in a medical context, specifically in the context of survival probability predictions.|Here is another description: The compatibility score is a numerical value that represents the compatibility between a donor and a recipient in a medical context, specifically in the context of survival probability predictions."},
{"compatibility_score__is_computed_by__obtaining_estimates_of_the_survival_probability": "The compatibility score is computed by a process that involves obtaining estimates of the survival probability at discrete time points using a trained model, which in this case is an XGBoost survival model."},
{"obtaining_estimates_of_the_survival_probability": "Obtaining estimates of the survival probability involves using a trained XGBoost survival model to predict the survival probability at discrete time points for an individual donor-recipient pair."},
{"survival_probability": "The survival probability is an estimate of the likelihood that an individual will survive past a certain time point. In the context of donor-recipient compatibility, it is used to predict the survival outcomes for a given pair.|Here is another description: The survival probability is a measure used to estimate the likelihood of an individual surviving past a certain time point. In the context of donor-recipient compatibility, it is crucial for predicting outcomes and making informed decisions.|Here is another description: The survival probability is an estimate of the likelihood that an individual will survive past a certain time point, calculated using a trained XGBoost survival model."},
{"survival_probability__is_obtained_using__trained_xgboost_survival_model": "The phrase \"is obtained using\" indicates the method or tool employed to derive the survival probability. Here, it specifies that the survival probability is calculated through the application of a trained XGBoost survival model."},
{"trained_xgboost_survival_model": "The trained XGBoost survival model is a machine learning model that has been specifically trained to predict survival probabilities. It uses the XGBoost algorithm, which is known for its high performance in various predictive modeling tasks, including survival analysis."},
{"survival_probability__is_represented_as__\u015d_pij_at_discrete_time_point_j": "The phrase \"is represented as\" indicates that the survival probability is mathematically or computationally expressed in a specific form. This representation allows for the quantification and analysis of survival likelihoods over discrete time intervals."},
{"\u015d_pij_at_discrete_time_point_j": "\u015c_pij_ at discrete time point _j_ represents the estimated survival probability for an individual donor-recipient pair at a specific time point. This estimation is derived using a trained XGBoost survival model, providing a numerical value that reflects the survival likelihood at that particular moment."},
{"survival_probability_si_tj": "The survival probability _Si_ ( _tj_ ) represents the estimated likelihood of survival at a specific discrete time point _j_ for an individual donor-recipient pair _i_, as predicted by a trained XGBoost survival model."},
{"survival_probability_si_tj__is_equal_to__\u015d_pij_for_j_1_2_m": "The predicate \"is equal to\" indicates that the survival probability _Si_ ( _tj_ ) is directly equated to the predicted survival probability \u015c_pij_ at each discrete time point _j_."},
{"\u015d_pij_for_j_1_2_m": "\u015c_pij_ represents the predicted survival probability at discrete time point _j_ for an individual donor-recipient pair _i_, as estimated by the XGBoost survival model. This value is used as the survival probability _Si_ ( _tj_ ) in the computation of the compatibility score."},
{"expected_survival_time": "The expected survival time is a statistical estimate of the average time a subject is expected to survive after a certain event or treatment, based on survival analysis models.|Here is another description: Expected survival time refers to the predicted duration for which a recipient is expected to survive post-transplantation. This metric is crucial in assessing the success and viability of the transplantation, as it provides an estimate of the longevity of the transplanted organ within the recipient.|Here is another description: The expected survival time is a statistical measure that represents the average time an individual is expected to survive after a certain event or diagnosis. It is a crucial metric in fields like medicine and actuarial science for predicting outcomes and planning interventions."},
{"expected_survival_time__is_approximated_by__discretely_integrating_the_survival_curve": "The phrase \"is approximated by\" indicates that the expected survival time is estimated using a specific method or formula, in this case, by discretely integrating the survival curve."},
{"discretely_integrating_the_survival_curve": "Discretely integrating the survival curve involves summing the survival probabilities at discrete time points, assuming the survival probability is constant within each interval, to estimate the total expected survival time."},
{"starting_time_point": "The starting time point is the initial moment in a sequence of time points used for calculating survival probabilities and expected survival times in a donor-recipient compatibility assessment."},
{"starting_time_point__is_defined_as__t_0_0": "The phrase \"is defined as\" indicates that the starting time point is explicitly set and characterized by a specific value within the context of survival analysis and compatibility scoring."},
{"t_0_0": "The object \"_t_ 0 = 0\" represents the initial time point in the survival analysis, where the survival probability is assumed to be 1, marking the beginning of the time series used for computing the compatibility score."},
{"starting_survival_probability": "The starting survival probability is the initial point in the survival curve analysis, representing the probability of survival at the beginning of the observation period."},
{"starting_survival_probability__is_defined_as__si_t_0_1": "The phrase \"is defined as\" indicates that the starting survival probability is explicitly set or established as a specific value within the context of survival analysis."},
{"si_t_0_1": "The object \"Si_ ( _t_ 0) = 1\" represents the initial survival probability at time zero, which is defined as 1, indicating a 100% probability of survival at the start of the observation period."},
{"survival_probability__is_assumed_constant_for__each_interval_tj_tj_1": "The phrase \"is assumed constant for\" indicates that within the specified intervals, the survival probability is treated as a stable, unchanging value for the purpose of calculation."},
{"each_interval_tj_tj_1": "Each interval [ _tj, tj_ +1] represents a discrete segment of time in the survival analysis, where _tj_ is the starting time point and _tj_ +1 is the end time point of the interval."},
{"survival_probability_for_interval_tj_tj_1": "The survival probability for the interval [ _tj, tj_ +1] represents the likelihood that an individual will survive from time _tj_ to time _tj_ +1. This probability is crucial in assessing the expected survival time over discrete intervals."},
{"survival_probability_for_interval_tj_tj_1__is_equal_to__s_tj_1": "The predicate \"is equal to\" indicates that the survival probability for the interval [ _tj, tj_ +1] is directly equated to the survival probability at time _tj_ +1. This assumption simplifies the calculation by treating the survival probability as constant within the interval."},
{"s_tj_1": "The object _S_ ( _tj_ +1) is the survival probability at the end of the interval _tj_ +1. It is used as a constant value to represent the survival probability throughout the interval [ _tj, tj_ +1], facilitating the discrete integration of the survival curve."},
{"incremental_contribution_for_one_time_interval": "The incremental contribution for one time interval refers to the additional amount of survival probability contributed within a specific time interval in the context of estimating expected survival time. It is a key component in the discrete integration of the survival curve to approximate the overall expected survival time for an individual."},
{"incremental_contribution_for_one_time_interval__is_represented_as__si_tj_1_tj_1_tj": "The phrase \"is represented as\" indicates the mathematical expression used to symbolize the incremental contribution for one time interval. It serves as a bridge between the conceptual understanding of incremental contribution and its quantitative representation in the form of a product of survival probability and time interval length."},
{"si_tj_1_tj_1_tj": "The object _Si_ ( _tj_ +1) \u00b7 ( _tj_ +1 \u2212 _tj_) represents the mathematical formulation of the incremental contribution for one time interval. It is the product of the survival probability at the end of the interval _Si_ ( _tj_ +1) and the length of the interval ( _tj_ +1 \u2212 _tj_), encapsulating the contribution of that interval to the overall expected survival time."},
{"compatibility_score_for_individual_i": "The compatibility score for individual _i_ is a measure used to evaluate the compatibility between a donor and a recipient in a medical context, specifically focusing on survival probabilities over time."},
{"compatibility_score_for_individual_i__is_named__discscore_i": "The predicate \"is named\" indicates that the compatibility score for individual _i_ has been given a specific designation or label to identify it within the context of the study or model."},
{"discscore_i": "_DisCScore_ ( _i_) is the designated name for the compatibility score of individual _i_, representing the computed measure of compatibility based on survival probabilities and expected survival time."},
{"compatibility_score__is_obtained_by__summing_the_increments_over_all_intervals": "The compatibility score is obtained by summing the increments over all intervals, which involves calculating the incremental contributions for each time interval based on survival probabilities and then aggregating these contributions."},
