{"we__refined__the_feature_set": "The predicate \"refined\" indicates the action taken by the subject to improve or make more precise the feature set through a series of assessments and expert reviews."},
{"the_feature_set": "The object \"the feature set\" represents the collection of variables or attributes that were subjected to refinement, including the removal of features with high missingness or low variability, and the inclusion of clinically important features as determined by expert clinicians."},
{"refinement": "The refinement refers to the process of improving or enhancing the feature set used in a study or analysis. In this context, it involves making adjustments to the initial set of features to better suit the objectives of the research."},
{"refinement__was_done_through__a_series_of_statistical_assessments": "The phrase \"was done through\" indicates the method or process by which the refinement of the feature set was achieved. It suggests that a specific approach or set of techniques was employed to carry out the refinement."},
{"a_series_of_statistical_assessments": "A series of statistical assessments refers to a collection of analytical methods and tests used to evaluate the features within the dataset. These assessments help determine the relevance, reliability, and quality of each feature, guiding decisions on which features to retain or discard during the refinement process."},
{"features": "The features in question are elements of a dataset that underwent a cleaning and refinement process, as part of a methodology inspired by Berrevoets et al. These features represent various data points or variables within the dataset that were subject to evaluation and potential removal based on certain criteria.|Here is another description: The features refer to the top 20 most frequently selected attributes in the _DiscSurvBoost_ model, which are crucial for its decision-making process in the context of transplant compatibility."},
{"features__were_removed_if__they_had_more_than_20_missingness_or_exhibited_zero_or_near_zero_variability": "The predicate \"were removed if\" signifies a conditional action taken during the data cleaning process. It indicates that certain features were excluded from the dataset based on specific conditions or criteria that were assessed during the refinement stage."},
{"they_had_more_than_20_missingness_or_exhibited_zero_or_near_zero_variability": "The object \"they had more than 20% missingness or exhibited zero or near-zero variability\" describes the specific conditions under which features were removed from the dataset. Features with more than 20% missing data points or those that showed little to no variation across the dataset were considered unsuitable for analysis and were therefore eliminated."},
{"expert_clinicians__reviewed__the_remaining_variables": "The act of reviewing by expert clinicians involves a thorough examination and assessment of the variables to ensure their clinical relevance and importance. This process helps in identifying features that are significant for the study and discarding those that are not."},
{"the_remaining_variables": "The remaining variables refer to the set of features that survived initial data cleaning and statistical assessments. These variables are then subjected to further scrutiny by expert clinicians to determine their clinical significance."},
{"expert_clinicians__identified__clinically_important_features": "The predicate \"identified\" signifies the action taken by the expert clinicians to recognize and select specific elements from a set of variables. This process involves careful evaluation and judgment to determine the significance of each variable in a clinical context."},
{"clinically_important_features": "Clinically important features refer to the variables or characteristics within a dataset that hold significant value for medical diagnosis, treatment, or research. These features are essential for making informed clinical decisions and are distinguished from irrelevant or less significant variables through expert review and statistical assessments."},
{"expert_clinicians__discarded__irrelevant_ones": "The predicate \"discarded\" indicates the action taken by the expert clinicians to remove or eliminate certain elements from consideration. In this context, it refers to the process of identifying and removing irrelevant features or variables from the dataset."},
{"irrelevant_ones": "The object \"irrelevant ones\" refers to the features or variables that were deemed irrelevant or unimportant by the expert clinicians during the feature selection process. These irrelevant features were discarded to refine the dataset and focus on clinically important information."},
{"the_full_inclusion_and_exclusion_criteria": "The full inclusion and exclusion criteria refer to the comprehensive set of rules and conditions that determine which study samples and features are selected for analysis. These criteria are essential for ensuring that the data used in the study is relevant, reliable, and clinically meaningful."},
{"the_full_inclusion_and_exclusion_criteria__are_shown_in__supplementary_a": "The phrase \"are shown in\" indicates that the detailed information about the inclusion and exclusion criteria can be found within a specified document or section. This predicate serves as a reference point, directing readers to the exact location where the criteria are outlined."},
{"supplementary_a": "Supplementary A is an additional document or section that accompanies the main text. It provides detailed information, such as the full inclusion and exclusion criteria for study samples and feature selection, which are crucial for understanding the methodology and ensuring reproducibility of the study."},
{"c_index": "The C-index, also known as Harrell\u2019s Concordance Index, is a metric specifically used in survival analysis to assess the discriminative ability of a model. It measures how well the model can distinguish between patients who will experience an event (like disease progression) sooner versus later.|Here is another description: The C-index, also known as Harrell\u2019s Concordance Index, is a metric used to evaluate the discriminative ability of a model in survival analysis. It measures how well the model can distinguish between patients who will experience an event sooner versus later.|Here is another description: The C-index, also known as Harrell's C-statistic, is a measure of the discriminatory power of a predictive model, particularly in survival analysis. It indicates the model's ability to distinguish between events occurring at different times."},
{"c_index__is_a_metric_for__evaluating_a_model_s_discriminative_ability": "\"Is a metric for\" indicates that the C-index serves as a quantitative measure to evaluate a specific aspect of a model's performance. In this context, it specifically assesses the model's ability to discriminate or differentiate between different outcomes or events over time."},
{"evaluating_a_model_s_discriminative_ability": "Evaluating a model\u2019s discriminative ability refers to the process of assessing how effectively a model can distinguish between different classes or outcomes. In the context of survival analysis, this means determining how well the model can predict which patient is more likely to experience an event (such as disease progression) sooner than another."},
{"c_index__is_used_in__survival_analysis": "The predicate \"is used in\" indicates that the C-index serves as a tool or metric within the broader field or application of survival analysis. It highlights the C-index's role and relevance in assessing model performance in this specific context."},
{"values_of_c_index_near_0_5": "Values of C-index near 0.5 refer to a specific range of the Harrell\u2019s Concordance Index, a metric used in survival analysis to evaluate a model\u2019s ability to discriminate between different outcomes over time."},
{"values_of_c_index_near_0_5__indicate__score_predictions_are_no_better_than_a_coin_flip": "The predicate \"indicate\" in this context means to show or suggest a particular situation or condition based on the observed values of the C-index."},
{"score_predictions_are_no_better_than_a_coin_flip": "Score predictions being no better than a coin flip means that the model's predictions are as accurate as random chance, suggesting that the model does not have any meaningful discriminative ability in determining which patient will live longer."},
{"values_of_c_index_near_1": "Values of C-index near 1 refer to a high level of Harrell\u2019s Concordance Index, which is a metric used in survival analysis to evaluate a model\u2019s ability to discriminate between patients based on their likelihood of experiencing an event, such as disease onset."},
{"values_of_c_index_near_1__indicate__scores_are_good_at_determining_which_of_two_patients_will_have_the_disease_first": "The predicate \"indicate\" in this context means that the observed values of the C-index provide a clear signal or suggestion about the effectiveness of the model\u2019s predictions. Specifically, it suggests how well the model can determine the order of events for patients."},
{"scores_are_good_at_determining_which_of_two_patients_will_have_the_disease_first": "Scores that are good at determining which of two patients will have the disease first signify a model with strong discriminative ability. This means the model can accurately predict which patient is more likely to experience the disease earlier based on the assigned scores."},
{"time_dependent_roc_curve": "The Time-Dependent ROC curve is a metric used in survival analysis to evaluate a model's discriminative ability at a specific time point _t_. It is a variation of the traditional ROC curve adapted for time-dependent data.|Here is another description: The Time-Dependent ROC curve is a method used in survival analysis to evaluate the discriminative ability of a model at a specific time point. It compares individuals who experience an event (such as failure or death) by a certain time _t_ with those who survive beyond that time."},
{"time_dependent_roc_curve__compares__individuals_who_fail_by_t_with_those_who_survive_beyond_t": "The predicate \"compares\" in this context signifies the action of the Time-Dependent ROC curve in assessing and differentiating between two groups of individuals based on their survival status at a specific time _t_."},
{"individuals_who_fail_by_t_with_those_who_survive_beyond_t": "The object \"individuals who fail by _t_ with those who survive beyond _t_\" refers to the two distinct groups of individuals that the Time-Dependent ROC curve evaluates. One group consists of individuals who experience the event of interest (failure) by time _t_, while the other group includes those who remain event-free beyond time _t_."},
{"time_dependent_roc_curve__measures__model_discrimination_at_t": "The predicate \"measures\" indicates that the Time-Dependent ROC curve quantifies or assesses a specific aspect of the model's performance. In this context, it specifically evaluates how well the model can discriminate between individuals based on their event status at a particular time _t_."},
{"model_discrimination_at_t": "Model discrimination at _t_ refers to the ability of the model to distinguish between individuals who will experience an event by time _t_ and those who will survive beyond _t_. This discrimination is quantified by the area under the Time-Dependent ROC curve at time _t_, known as AUC(_t_), which indicates the model's effectiveness in assigning higher risk scores to individuals who fail earlier."},
{"auc_t": "AUC( _t_ ) is a metric used in survival analysis to evaluate a model's discriminative ability at a specific time _t_. It is derived from the time-dependent ROC curve, which compares individuals who fail by time _t_ with those who survive beyond _t_."},
{"auc_t__quantifies__the_probability_that_the_model_assigns_a_higher_risk_score_to_the_individual_who_fails_earlier": "The predicate \"quantifies\" indicates that AUC( _t_ ) provides a numerical measure of the model's performance. Specifically, it quantifies the likelihood that the model correctly assigns a higher risk score to individuals who fail earlier."},
{"the_probability_that_the_model_assigns_a_higher_risk_score_to_the_individual_who_fails_earlier": "The object \"the probability that the model assigns a higher risk score to the individual who fails earlier\" refers to the likelihood that the model's risk scores are aligned with the actual outcomes. A higher AUC( _t_ ) value indicates a greater probability that the model can distinguish between individuals who fail earlier and those who survive longer."},
{"auc_t_of_0_5": "AUC( _t_ ) of 0.5 refers to the Area Under the Curve at a specific time _t_, which is a measure used in survival analysis to evaluate the discriminative ability of a model at that particular time point."},
{"auc_t_of_0_5__indicates__random_guessing_at_time_t": "The predicate \"indicates\" in this context signifies that a specific value of AUC( _t_ ) points towards a certain level of model performance. In this case, it shows that the model's predictions are akin to random guessing at time _t_."},
{"random_guessing_at_time_t": "Random guessing at time _t_ means that the model's predictions at that specific time point are no better than chance. An AUC( _t_ ) value of 0.5 suggests that the model cannot reliably distinguish between individuals who will fail by time _t_ and those who will survive beyond _t_."},
{"auc_t_near_1_0": "AUC( _t_ ) near 1.0 refers to the area under the time-dependent ROC curve at a specific time _t_, which is a measure used in survival analysis to evaluate a model's ability to discriminate between individuals who will fail (e.g., experience an event) by time _t_ and those who will survive beyond _t_."},
{"auc_t_near_1_0__signifies__strong_discriminative_ability": "The predicate \"signifies\" in this context means that the value of AUC( _t_ ) near 1.0 is an indicator or a clear signal of a particular characteristic or capability of the model, which in this case is its discriminative ability."},
{"strong_discriminative_ability": "Strong discriminative ability refers to the capability of a model to effectively distinguish between different classes or outcomes, in this context, between individuals who will fail by time _t_ and those who will survive beyond _t_. A model with strong discriminative ability can accurately predict which individuals are at higher risk of the event occurring earlier."},
